WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:03.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:04.679
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
Hi, everyone.

00:00:04.679 --> 00:00:05.970
My name is Peter-Mark Verwoerd.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.510
I'm a solution architect
for Google Cloud.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:14.260
I focus on migration solutions
and help customers basically

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.450
discover what Google
Cloud is and how to use it

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:17.914
and how to move to it.

00:00:17.914 --> 00:00:18.580
BRIAN STARK: Hi.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:19.120
I'm Brian Stark.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.160
I'm a product manager at
Google Cloud platform.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.843
I'm responsible for
basically the same topic.

00:00:22.843 --> 00:00:23.342
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:23.342 --> 00:00:25.570
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
So the two of us

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.070
end up talking to
a lot of customers

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:31.480
about how Google
Cloud works for them

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.910
and how they can
migrate to Google Cloud

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:38.150
and what are the best
practices, how to do it,

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:40.412
and what are the
motivations and what

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:41.620
are the consequences of that.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:44.770
So what we'd like to do for the
next hour or maybe a little bit

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:46.904
less is talk to you a
little bit about some

00:00:46.904 --> 00:00:48.820
of the common scenarios
and some of the things

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.380
that we see in migrations.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:57.180
Where the title comes from of a
single migrated application is,

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.620
I think, a lot of
the time when we

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.120
go into customers
I think there is

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.746
a bit of an uncertainty to the
scope of the problem, the size

00:01:05.746 --> 00:01:06.370
of the problem.

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:07.870
And so we thought
we'd bring it down

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:09.599
to the scale of a
single application.

00:01:09.599 --> 00:01:11.640
What does it look like to
do the one application,

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:12.930
because I think that's
much more manageable

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.910
and conceptually it's
easier to handle.

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:19.160
So you've made
this big decision.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.780
Yay, we're going to the cloud.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.070
Now what?

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:25.780
You've got a data center.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.564
Inside that data center you
got services, applications,

00:01:29.564 --> 00:01:31.480
something somebody wrote
about four years ago.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:32.570
Nobody knows how it works.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.840
You've got databases
that are running,

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.490
storage media that
you're running.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.770
Is that a Solaris box
in the background?

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.870
I don't even know
what's going on.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:42.550
At a certain point
you start feeling

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.530
overwhelmed with all the
stuff that's in there.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:46.790
Do you want to give up?

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.390
Do you to just say no?

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:51.820
BRIAN STARK: Stop!

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:52.950
Don't give up.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.450
As it turns out migrating to
the Cloud can be a lot of work,

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.430
but it doesn't have to
be overwhelming work.

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:58.930
We've worked with
a lot of customers

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:01.192
in migrating that can be
a straightforward process.

00:02:01.192 --> 00:02:02.650
Migration has
parallels to building

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.550
a house, which is probably why
we use this analogy so often.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:07.330
And you're going to
move into this house.

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.590
But before you can
move into your house,

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.750
you have to put the things
that the house needs,

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:12.670
like a plumbing
and a foundation.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:14.586
Otherwise, you're just
dropping your furniture

00:02:14.586 --> 00:02:17.534
into an empty lot, and
we don't want to do that.

00:02:17.534 --> 00:02:18.950
So before we move
into this house,

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.241
we need to put in the plumbing.

00:02:20.241 --> 00:02:22.624
This is what I like to refer
to as the Google plumbing.

00:02:22.624 --> 00:02:24.610
You need to have
a Google account.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.640
You need to create
an organization.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.010
You need to set up the billing.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:30.468
These are all basics you need
to do before a single VM gets

00:02:30.468 --> 00:02:30.969
started.

00:02:30.969 --> 00:02:33.009
And while some of this is
Google account related,

00:02:33.009 --> 00:02:35.480
there are also some things you
need to decide as a policy.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:36.530
Of course, the first
thing you need to do

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:37.905
is create a Google
Cloud account.

00:02:37.905 --> 00:02:39.530
There's much more to
be done before you

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:40.790
can start moving application.

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:44.010
Let's go into the rest
of this individually.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:46.544
The most important bit of Google
plumbing to set up is IAM.

00:02:46.544 --> 00:02:48.210
IAM can be complicated,
but it basically

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.830
comes down to what can you do.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:51.720
Who can do something?

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.947
An identity.

00:02:52.947 --> 00:02:53.655
What can they do?

00:02:53.655 --> 00:02:54.320
A policy.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:54.820
To what?

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:55.590
A resource.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.090
It's important to
decide who will be

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.890
able to do what when you start.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.949
It may not be exactly
the right kind,

00:03:00.949 --> 00:03:02.490
but it's better than
having no policy

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:03.870
and implementing it later.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.286
Extending your analogy is kind
of like installing plumbing

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:09.300
after your house has been built.
It's possible, but it's messy.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.485
So how do we set this up?

00:03:12.485 --> 00:03:14.100
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
This is actually

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.590
one of my colleagues,
Vic, very animatedly

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.500
talking about the various ways
that customers can actually

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.687
set up the various aspects
of the Google plumbing

00:03:22.687 --> 00:03:23.520
before they move in.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:30.390
And I love his animations, so
I shamelessly steal his gif.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:33.000
So the reality is
there's actually no one

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.560
way of doing this precisely.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.040
There's no one formula that
fits every single customer.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.140
But we do offer, when
we speak to customers

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.580
who have these kinds of
questions about setting up

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.470
before they start
migrating-- so there

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.080
are a lot of best practices
that we can recommend.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.690
So going through a few of
these, certainly principle

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.870
of least privilege is something
that we preach relatively

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.170
constantly to everyone.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.370
The idea of handing out only
what permissions people need

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:04.112
in order to accomplish the
job that they're doing,

00:04:04.112 --> 00:04:06.320
rather than handing out
permissions indiscriminately.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:07.940
You don't want to
necessarily make

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.520
all your developers owners
of every single project

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.830
or owners of the
entire organization.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:16.880
You want to kind of restrict
that permission, like ownership

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:18.380
of a development
project or being

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.871
able to deploy but not
necessarily to terminate,

00:04:20.871 --> 00:04:21.829
all these other things.

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:25.160
Or, for example, like
billing ownership and being

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:26.990
able to determine
where the bill goes

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:28.240
and what projects are billing.

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.340
These are important permissions
that you want to appropriately

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:32.550
assign as necessary.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.050
So you don't want to restrict
somebody from doing their job,

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.049
but you also don't want
to necessarily give them

00:04:37.049 --> 00:04:39.470
more permission than they
need to be able to do it.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:45.710
Secondly, using groups actually
makes managing permissions

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:49.580
much, much easier than having
to manage individual permissions

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:50.810
on their own.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.724
Having to manage
individual identities

00:04:52.724 --> 00:04:54.140
and send out
individual identities

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:57.710
and switching roles and then
switching what they can do

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:01.280
and what they can't do, it's
operationally more burdensome

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.629
and it leads to things
falling through the cracks.

00:05:03.629 --> 00:05:06.170
Being able to use groups means
that you can assign permission

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:06.740
to groups.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.365
In long term, these
groups are supposed

00:05:08.365 --> 00:05:09.722
to be able to do things--

00:05:09.722 --> 00:05:11.180
people come and go
from the groups,

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.680
but their permissions
don't move.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:15.920
So for example, let's say you
had a network security group.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.500
They are responsible for setting
all the firewall rules, all

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.570
the networking rules,
when networks are created

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.430
and destroyed, and so on.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:25.130
Rather than assigning
that bit of permission,

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:27.440
those permission
sets to individuals

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.840
when they join the
network security team,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.030
you assign that to the group.

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:33.652
People come and go
within that team,

00:05:33.652 --> 00:05:35.360
but the permissions
never have to change.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:42.580
It's a lot less to manage, and
it's a lot less error prone.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.690
Similarly, actually controlling
who can set the policy--

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:49.570
so the watchers of the
watchers as it were--

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:50.620
is very important.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.041
Being able to determine
what group of people

00:05:53.041 --> 00:05:55.540
are going to say, yes, these
are the appropriate permissions

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:56.860
for this group of
people, and that's

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.068
the appropriate permissions
for that group of people.

00:05:59.068 --> 00:06:01.930
It's very important to
be able to manage that

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:02.990
at a very granular level.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:04.906
You don't want to hand
that kind of permission

00:06:04.906 --> 00:06:06.562
out to a lot of
different people.

00:06:06.562 --> 00:06:08.020
That should be a
smaller group that

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:11.320
has the authority, that has
the institutional process

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:15.100
and know how as to who
should be assigned to what

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.389
and be able to do what.

00:06:16.389 --> 00:06:17.930
So it's a very small
group of people,

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:21.360
but you want to make sure
it's fairly restricted.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:26.740
And another way to increase
your permission setup

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.630
before you start
moving to Google Cloud

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:32.380
is to make sure to invest
in looking at audit logs.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.360
This is a part of Stackdriver.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.970
We audit every single
API call that you make.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:39.377
Look at these audit logs,
start using these audit logs,

00:06:39.377 --> 00:06:40.960
and be familiar with
these audit logs,

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.543
because you want to
know what is being

00:06:42.543 --> 00:06:50.860
done at any given point within
the overall Google environment.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:54.477
And then this was something
else Brian briefly touched on--

00:06:54.477 --> 00:06:55.810
is setting up your organization.

00:06:55.810 --> 00:06:58.062
So within Google
Cloud you can use

00:06:58.062 --> 00:06:59.770
what we call Cloud
resource manager to be

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:01.436
able to create an
organization that maps

00:07:01.436 --> 00:07:03.190
to your literal organization.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.320
So at the top, there
is this concept

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:09.100
of an organizational node
and billing can flow up

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:10.660
and permissions flow down.

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.030
And so being able to set
this up from the start,

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.910
before you start moving things,
before you start instantiating

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:17.702
virtual machines,
databases, what have you,

00:07:17.702 --> 00:07:18.868
is really, really important.

00:07:18.868 --> 00:07:21.040
Again, it's the kind of
thing you could do later on,

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.210
but it's messy.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.000
And so there are a
couple of different ways

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.700
that people take the
organization concept

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:30.122
and decide how to use the
projects underneath it.

00:07:30.122 --> 00:07:32.080
I'm going to walk through
three of these really

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:34.551
briefly, because
the first concept is

00:07:34.551 --> 00:07:35.800
the easiest one to understand.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:37.630
All right, we're going to have
one organization, one project,

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:39.506
everyone's going to swim
inside this big pool

00:07:39.506 --> 00:07:41.046
of the single project,
and everything

00:07:41.046 --> 00:07:42.070
gets deployed in there.

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:45.370
And conceptually that's really
easy to manage and figure out,

00:07:45.370 --> 00:07:50.020
but the downside is actually
quotas and permissions

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.130
are tied to the project.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:55.276
So let's say you've
got two teams that

00:07:55.276 --> 00:07:56.650
want to use
databases, and you've

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.775
allocated quota for
Bigtable to one team,

00:07:58.775 --> 00:08:00.400
and the other team
just grabs it first.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:02.260
Well, within the
construct of the project

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:03.610
there's nothing
really preventing them

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:04.276
from doing that.

00:08:04.276 --> 00:08:06.700
So you end up getting a
lot of quota contention

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:11.170
if a lot of different teams
are sharing the same project.

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:13.420
The next option is kind
of to divide up, say,

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.790
along divisional lines
or broader groups, which

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:16.880
is a little bit easier.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.140
You get less contention
over resources,

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:22.180
but it kind of fairly
quickly devolves

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:23.980
into what the first
option looks like,

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:27.370
where it's not really
enough separation to make

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:28.709
anyone happy.

00:08:28.709 --> 00:08:30.250
What we generally
recommend, and this

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:31.916
is kind of how Google
works, is actually

00:08:31.916 --> 00:08:36.610
projects divided up by kind
of the feature or the product

00:08:36.610 --> 00:08:37.570
level.

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:39.640
So a product team
or a feature team

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:42.520
would own a particular
project for their own feature

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:43.826
in their own project.

00:08:43.826 --> 00:08:45.700
They get quota based on
what they need to do.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.366
They're not sharing
it with other teams.

00:08:47.366 --> 00:08:50.020
And that's kind of the level
of ownership that we recommend.

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:51.550
What is also helpful
at this level

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.550
is that not only are
quotas divided by products

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:55.500
but permissions.

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.670
So permissions can
filter down from the top

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.460
and be able to be separated
out at the project level, which

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.100
is also very handy for being
able to kind of appropriately

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.210
divide up different teams.

00:09:09.310 --> 00:09:10.140
So there you go.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.016
BRIAN STARK: And finally,
what considerations

00:09:12.016 --> 00:09:12.931
are there for regions?

00:09:12.931 --> 00:09:14.460
If you guys watched
this morning,

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:16.293
they announced a whole
bunch of new regions.

00:09:16.293 --> 00:09:18.960
We're now up to, I think,
17 is what's been announced.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.294
But there can be regulatory,
political, or policy

00:09:21.294 --> 00:09:22.710
reasons why you
don't want to have

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:23.918
your data in certain regions.

00:09:23.918 --> 00:09:26.310
Some companies want to
keep their data in the US.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.560
Recently a lot more want
to keep it out of the US.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.101
Some want to have it in the EU,
some don't want it in the EU.

00:09:31.101 --> 00:09:33.812
So you need to have a policy
of where applications reside.

00:09:33.812 --> 00:09:36.270
You want to be as close to user
base as possible generally,

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:40.419
but you have to deal with
your policy compliance issues.

00:09:40.419 --> 00:09:42.460
And you also want to limit
the amount of overhead

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.610
in your IT team supporting
multiple regions.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:46.460
So all of these must
be considered before.

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:48.450
If you don't do this ahead of
time, what's going to happen

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.220
is your developers are
going to start picking

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:51.761
and then you'll be
stuck with regions

00:09:51.761 --> 00:09:54.870
sprawled all over the place.

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.010
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
All right, Brian.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:58.180
So we covered everything.

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:00.250
Now we can talk about
migrating something, right?

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:00.920
BRIAN STARK: We're
building a house.

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:01.400
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Yeah

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:02.350
BRIAN STARK: We put some
pipes in the ground,

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:03.790
and you're ready to move in?

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:05.123
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Can we not?

00:10:05.123 --> 00:10:06.400
BRIAN STARK: No, no.

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:08.483
We're not ready to move
at this point, Peter-Mark.

00:10:08.483 --> 00:10:09.970
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Fine.

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:10.900
BRIAN STARK: We have to
do the next part, which

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:11.650
is the foundation.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:12.959
It's a concrete slab.

00:10:12.959 --> 00:10:15.000
This is what sits on top
of the Google plumbing--

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.791
the decisions that come
from your processes

00:10:16.791 --> 00:10:17.890
and your company.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:19.660
What kind of network
are you going to use?

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:22.243
What kind of connectivity do we
have to existing data centers?

00:10:22.243 --> 00:10:24.010
What will your developer
workflow look be?

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:27.156
And we need to look
at each of these.

00:10:27.156 --> 00:10:31.060
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: So
unlike the product structure

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:34.630
where we generally recommend
that third option where

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:40.080
each team gets
their own project,

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:44.714
the network design is actually
something that could credibly

00:10:44.714 --> 00:10:47.130
say that we want to use a bunch
of these different options

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:48.520
or one of them.

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.296
This is not one of
those suggestions

00:10:50.296 --> 00:10:52.170
where this one is better
and that one is not.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:53.337
These are all kind of valid.

00:10:53.337 --> 00:10:55.295
So the first one that
we're going to talk about

00:10:55.295 --> 00:10:56.610
is basically network islands.

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:59.160
And that each project or
each service, whatever it is,

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:01.380
kind of lives within
its own isolated island.

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.570
And the only exposure it
has to the outside world

00:11:03.570 --> 00:11:06.750
is via an API or some sort
of consumable resource

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:10.170
that other projects, other
consumers, whether they're

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:11.587
internal or external, can hit.

00:11:11.587 --> 00:11:13.920
And as far as the rest of the
outside world is concerned

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:15.727
it's a black box.

00:11:15.727 --> 00:11:17.310
And so that kind of
connectivity means

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.819
that it's a very, very
high level of isolation.

00:11:21.819 --> 00:11:23.610
It's limiting the blast
radius for any kind

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:24.630
of issues that come up.

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:26.790
But it also means that it's--

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:29.520
you really need to be
very good at knowing what

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:31.944
your outside consumers,
whether they're colleagues,

00:11:31.944 --> 00:11:34.110
whether they are people
internally who are consuming

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:35.360
these resources or externally.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:38.550
You really need to
know what that is.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.810
Next option is a slight
variation on this--

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:42.810
doing network peering,
where you connect

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:46.890
different networks as necessary,
say, over direct links, though.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:50.062
So you connect the
network, say, over a VPN.

00:11:50.062 --> 00:11:52.020
And this is another model
that we see a lot of,

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.979
in particular with companies
that are creating networks

00:11:54.979 --> 00:11:56.520
for different groups
inside the cloud

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:58.811
but also want to connect back
to, say, an existing data

00:11:58.811 --> 00:12:01.200
center and be able to connect
back to networks there.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.210
This kind of fits in
with the mental model

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:04.690
of what they've already done.

00:12:04.690 --> 00:12:06.750
And so we see this a lot, too.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.300
A variation on that is doing
a network hub and spoke,

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:11.610
where, say, shared
services reside inside

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:13.230
of a single network.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:16.215
Maybe that's where the
connectivity from the office,

00:12:16.215 --> 00:12:18.590
or the existing data center,
or whatever the case may be,

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:19.673
is going to land in there.

00:12:19.673 --> 00:12:22.380
You've got shared
services like an LDAP

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:26.020
or an ID or something like that.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:28.620
And then the other networks
that are in the cloud kind

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.260
of just phone into
that central network

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:34.410
and consume whatever resources
they need but otherwise sit

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:36.944
in their own isolated network.

00:12:36.944 --> 00:12:38.610
And then something
that we actually just

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:41.145
launched publicly today is XPN.

00:12:41.145 --> 00:12:43.530
So that stands for
cross project network.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:47.680
And this is kind
of a variation on--

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:49.852
it's really not a variation
on anything, but--

00:12:49.852 --> 00:12:53.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:58.005
If you want all your resources
to reside in the same network,

00:12:58.005 --> 00:12:59.380
if you want all
of your projects,

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:00.820
if you want all
of your features,

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:02.940
if you want all your
services in the same project,

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:07.740
but what you want to avoid is
teams deciding on their own

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:10.920
what are the
network permissions,

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:13.440
what are the actions that
you're allowed to do,

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:15.330
what you're not allowed to do,
where the route is going to go,

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:16.580
and all those sorts of things.

00:13:16.580 --> 00:13:19.200
What you can do is
create an XPN network,

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:22.110
where the ownership
of that XPN network

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:23.935
is for a networking team.

00:13:23.935 --> 00:13:25.810
They are going to decide
all the permissions.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:28.435
They're going to decide with the
firewalls look like and so on.

00:13:28.435 --> 00:13:30.870
And then they grant permission
to other projects to say,

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.450
you are granted permission
to instantiate resources

00:13:33.450 --> 00:13:34.424
within this XPN.

00:13:34.424 --> 00:13:36.090
And you're going to
own those resources.

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:38.380
You're going to own those
virtual machines and so on.

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.380
But you're not going
to own the network.

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.352
You're just going to
sit in the network,

00:13:42.352 --> 00:13:44.310
and we're going to manage
that network for you.

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:46.950
And so that lets you have
a shared global network

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:50.130
or a shared regional network,
depending on what you want,

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:51.810
without having to
share out permissions

00:13:51.810 --> 00:13:56.510
to managing the database
or managing the network.

00:13:56.510 --> 00:13:59.050
So next, this is
very much related

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:03.970
to what the network design looks
like, what kind of connectivity

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:05.580
will you have to
existing resources?

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:06.955
So we're talking
about migration.

00:14:06.955 --> 00:14:09.329
There's an existing
resource somewhere.

00:14:09.329 --> 00:14:10.870
And how do you want
to connect to it?

00:14:10.870 --> 00:14:12.250
And I think like
the network design,

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:13.330
there's not a right answer here.

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:14.590
There are just
different options.

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:17.006
So I think one of the first
options that most people think

00:14:17.006 --> 00:14:20.560
of is creating a VPN
tunnel, an encrypted site

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.990
to site and tunnel
between a data center

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:24.040
and up to Google Cloud.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:26.410
And I think a lot of
projects start that way.

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:31.150
The next level is leasing
out a line with a partner.

00:14:31.150 --> 00:14:34.330
So getting a certain amount
of guaranteed capacity

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:36.310
over a private line
connecting between Google

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:38.850
and yourself is
certainly an option.

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:40.660
And then finally,
actually, what is also

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:42.536
a totally valid option
is companies that say,

00:14:42.536 --> 00:14:43.118
you know what?

00:14:43.118 --> 00:14:44.900
We've got this stuff
in our data center,

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:45.941
and that's sitting there.

00:14:45.941 --> 00:14:47.800
What we're doing is
we are redeploying.

00:14:47.800 --> 00:14:52.804
We are moving applications not
by actual transferring of bits,

00:14:52.804 --> 00:14:54.220
but actually these
things are just

00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:56.650
going to be recreated
in the cloud.

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:59.380
We don't want to have that
connection between our existing

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.522
resources and our
ongoing resources.

00:15:01.522 --> 00:15:02.980
So there are
different ways to look

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:04.810
at this and different
kind of trade-offs.

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:06.190
And it kind of depends
very much on kind

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:07.570
of what you're looking
for and what you're trying

00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:08.920
to get out of a migration.

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:13.100
Also, not all of these
decisions have to be long term.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:17.240
They don't have to be the last
and final decision on this.

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:19.300
We also see a lot of
customers say, well,

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:20.980
while we're in our
transition phase,

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:22.355
we're going to
actually lease out

00:15:22.355 --> 00:15:24.996
a lot of capacity between
our data center and Google.

00:15:24.996 --> 00:15:26.620
So we can transfer
things more quickly,

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:29.700
we can have a wider
pipe into Google Cloud.

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.450
And then after we're
done, actually, we're

00:15:31.450 --> 00:15:32.770
just going to turn it off.

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:34.940
So you have two, three,
six months, 12 months,

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:37.600
whatever the case may
be of a lease line,

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:41.300
and then you turn
it off afterwards.

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:45.110
And then, finally, what does
this look like for developers

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:46.410
as they use the cloud?

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:48.745
So if you consider
a company when

00:15:48.745 --> 00:15:50.120
leadership has
made the decision,

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:51.920
yes, we're moving
to Google Cloud.

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:53.390
We're happy for
all these reasons.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:55.490
We've made our decision.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.900
Operations knows about all this.

00:15:57.900 --> 00:15:59.750
Say, OK, well, data
centers are closing down

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:00.583
for whatever reason.

00:16:00.583 --> 00:16:01.800
We're moving to cloud.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:03.710
There's a very good chance,
especially as organizations

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:05.990
grow, that not every developer
is going to know about this.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:08.030
Not every developer is going
to be in on every discussion,

00:16:08.030 --> 00:16:08.847
every decision.

00:16:08.847 --> 00:16:09.680
They're carrying on.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:11.120
They're doing their job.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:14.270
They're fixing bugs,
creating features,

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:16.670
releasing new products,
whatever the case may be.

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:18.270
They're not part
of this process.

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:21.870
And so understanding
that point of view

00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:25.620
and making sure that developers,
when they move to the cloud,

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:27.630
have a particular
workflow that makes sense.

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:29.910
And then this really,
again, comes down

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:32.340
to what a company wants to do.

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:36.300
How do you want to make that
workflow for your developers,

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:37.770
essentially internal customers?

00:16:37.770 --> 00:16:39.534
Are you going to
keep it the same

00:16:39.534 --> 00:16:41.700
and try to make all the
changes on your own back-end

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:45.880
so that whatever tooling they
use seems kind of seamless?

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:48.547
Like, oh, I used to be deploying
to a San Francisco data center,

00:16:48.547 --> 00:16:50.046
and now I'm deploying
to US central,

00:16:50.046 --> 00:16:51.480
otherwise my life is the same?

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:52.440
Or are you saying,
you know what?

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:53.481
We're taking this chance.

00:16:53.481 --> 00:16:55.512
We're going to change
everything that we did.

00:16:55.512 --> 00:16:56.720
We don't like the old system.

00:16:56.720 --> 00:16:57.470
Here is the new system.

00:16:57.470 --> 00:16:58.428
We're going to do that.

00:16:58.428 --> 00:17:01.940
And these all come with kind
of varying levels of effort

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:03.360
and operational
overhead depending

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:04.360
on where you want to go.

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:06.960
If you want to do
a lot of homework

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:09.780
for your operations team to
make it easier for feature

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.160
developers, you can do that.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:12.180
Or if you want to
say, you know what?

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:13.304
We're all in the same boat.

00:17:13.304 --> 00:17:14.910
We're making this
big transition.

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:16.859
Feature developers are going
to help out the operations team

00:17:16.859 --> 00:17:17.670
as well--

00:17:17.670 --> 00:17:23.099
and varying levels of
effort in between there.

00:17:23.099 --> 00:17:27.660
So if you're not already
using some sort of automation

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:31.350
pipeline, if you're not
using continuous integration,

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:33.960
continuous deployment, this is
actually a great opportunity

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:37.960
to start using it, because it
makes migrations much, much,

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:39.930
much easier.

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:42.300
If you're already using it,
please continue to do so.

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:45.400
I'm sure you've already noticed
that it makes your life easier.

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:48.030
And then finally with
compute, what we like to say

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:50.732
is when doing a
migration, don't try

00:17:50.732 --> 00:17:51.940
to change everything at once.

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:55.290
So try to do something like
for like as much as possible.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:57.160
So if you're running
on bare metal,

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:59.700
if you're using virtualized
machines, continue to do that.

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:03.510
Use virtual machines and
try to compare or find

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:06.154
comparable versions
among instance types.

00:18:06.154 --> 00:18:08.070
But I think one thing
that's important to look

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:10.620
at when you do that is look
at the actual resources you're

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:15.040
consuming on a virtual machine
rather than ones that you have.

00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:18.479
So if you've got a
64 core box that's

00:18:18.479 --> 00:18:20.520
running whatever application,
but you're actually

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:26.460
only at like 10% CPU usage, you
may not need a 64 core machine.

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:28.440
And then finally, if
you're using containers,

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:29.890
please, again,
continue to do so.

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:30.681
We like them a lot.

00:18:30.681 --> 00:18:33.170
They run pretty well.

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:35.449
All right, Brian, that's
got to be it, right?

00:18:35.449 --> 00:18:37.365
BRIAN STARK: We've got
pipes and a foundation.

00:18:37.365 --> 00:18:37.920
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Yeah.

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:38.980
BRIAN STARK: And
you want to move on?

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:39.320
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Yeah.

00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:40.830
BRIAN STARK: So you're
on the sales team, right?

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:41.871
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Yes.

00:18:41.871 --> 00:18:42.820
BRIAN STARK: OK.

00:18:42.820 --> 00:18:43.860
On the engineering
side of the house,

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:45.000
we like to do a little bit more.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:46.208
No, you're not ready to move.

00:18:46.208 --> 00:18:47.940
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Man.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:48.440
Fine.

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:51.814
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:51.814 --> 00:18:53.020
BRIAN STARK: Yeah.

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:55.570
So we've built the plumbing
and the foundation, Peter-Mark.

00:18:55.570 --> 00:18:57.970
But the first application
needs to be selected.

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:00.207
What's going to move
into the house first?

00:19:00.207 --> 00:19:01.540
In theory, it could be anything.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:02.590
And there's a lot
of different points

00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:04.810
of view over what the first
thing you should move is.

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:06.130
I've talked to
some customers that

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:08.671
say it's only exciting when you
move the hardest thing first.

00:19:08.671 --> 00:19:11.530
I don't encourage that.

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:14.830
A lot of people prefer something
that's a little bit cleaner

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:17.284
and is going to create less
disruption in the business.

00:19:17.284 --> 00:19:18.700
So I'll talk about
commonalities--

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:22.030
not guarantees of what's
kind of a good, easy thing

00:19:22.030 --> 00:19:23.055
to move first.

00:19:23.055 --> 00:19:24.430
Generally you want
something that

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:25.596
has few if any dependencies.

00:19:25.596 --> 00:19:28.330
It's not talking to like
30 or 50 different machines

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:30.807
within the data center.

00:19:30.807 --> 00:19:32.890
You want something that's
running Linux or Windows

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:34.990
and fairly modern.

00:19:34.990 --> 00:19:37.504
If you're running Red
Hat 4 or Windows 2003,

00:19:37.504 --> 00:19:38.920
that's probably
not where you want

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:42.490
to start in terms of getting
your application over to Cloud.

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:45.130
And then this is surely
just recommendation, take it

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:46.090
for what it's worth--

00:19:46.090 --> 00:19:47.987
internal can be
better than external.

00:19:47.987 --> 00:19:50.320
Most companies would prefer
to aggravate their employees

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:51.460
than their customers.

00:19:51.460 --> 00:19:52.840
So if you want to move
an internal system that's

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:54.684
cut inward facing and
see if that works--

00:19:54.684 --> 00:19:56.350
it tends to be a
little bit better place

00:19:56.350 --> 00:20:01.232
to start than just your
web facing property.

00:20:01.232 --> 00:20:03.644
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: All right.

00:20:03.644 --> 00:20:05.060
I know you're going
to tell me no,

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:08.000
but we've got to be
ready to move now, right?

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:09.170
I mean, what else is there?

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:09.665
BRIAN STARK: We're ready.

00:20:09.665 --> 00:20:10.320
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Oh.

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:11.050
BRIAN STARK: You made it.

00:20:11.050 --> 00:20:12.430
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Finally.

00:20:12.430 --> 00:20:13.460
BRIAN STARK: You've
been so eager,

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:14.090
but we finally got there.

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:14.420
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: I know.

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:15.253
I just want to move.

00:20:15.253 --> 00:20:16.570
BRIAN STARK: We got the house.

00:20:16.570 --> 00:20:17.570
All right.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:20.210
So as long as you're running
a supported operating system

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:21.874
you should be fine.

00:20:21.874 --> 00:20:23.290
As long as this
is one application

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:26.090
it should be relatively
straightforward.

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:28.855
If you've got the CI/CD pipeline
that Peter-Mark talked about,

00:20:28.855 --> 00:20:29.480
that's awesome.

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:32.054
You can just redeploy to
a new target Google Cloud.

00:20:32.054 --> 00:20:33.845
We just announced our
VM migration service,

00:20:33.845 --> 00:20:36.320
so that's an awesome way
to move virtual machines

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:39.140
or physical machines if
you want to go that way

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:41.750
and just copy the
machine into the Cloud.

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:44.264
It makes things pretty easy
in terms of a migration.

00:20:44.264 --> 00:20:45.680
If the application
has a database,

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:47.162
move that at the same time.

00:20:47.162 --> 00:20:49.370
You're only doing one move
here, so the prudent thing

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:51.890
is to pause the application,
pick a downtime window,

00:20:51.890 --> 00:20:56.030
and just go slow and go
gradual and don't rush it.

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:58.215
If you guys get a
chance to talk to Lush,

00:20:58.215 --> 00:20:59.840
I think they moved
their entire website

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:01.100
in the course of 22 days.

00:21:01.100 --> 00:21:02.090
I don't encourage that.

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:06.500
It's an option, but it leads
to long nights and stress.

00:21:09.502 --> 00:21:11.710
Yeah, I'm going to give you
some best practices here.

00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:14.080
They're not in any
particular order.

00:21:14.080 --> 00:21:16.540
We put them down in the
way we thought to be best.

00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:19.010
So first stage is
the migrations.

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:20.400
Try to schedule it.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:23.000
Try to have a defined period
you're going to do this in.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:25.394
Don't try to rush this out
on artificial deadlines.

00:21:25.394 --> 00:21:26.810
Take your time and
do it correctly

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:30.200
as opposed to forcing it.

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:33.009
Create clear goals for your
migrating team, define success.

00:21:33.009 --> 00:21:34.550
Make it very clear
what you're trying

00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:37.370
to achieve so that when
you've done the migration

00:21:37.370 --> 00:21:41.430
you can say this was successful
or this was not successful.

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:43.150
And then you may wish
to offer guidance

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:45.070
on what kind of
instance types to use,

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:47.052
what kind of storage
products and databases.

00:21:47.052 --> 00:21:49.510
Typically you're going to find
your team isn't particularly

00:21:49.510 --> 00:21:50.890
familiar with Cloud.

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:54.010
So it's going be critical
that you give some guidance.

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:55.990
And then as you
develop this expertise,

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:57.070
make sure the teams
are transferring it

00:21:57.070 --> 00:21:58.060
from one to the
next so you don't

00:21:58.060 --> 00:21:59.170
have to reinvent the
wheel every time you

00:21:59.170 --> 00:22:00.910
try to move an
additional application

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:02.737
within your business.

00:22:02.737 --> 00:22:04.820
As Peter-Mark mentioned,
deployment tooling-- it's

00:22:04.820 --> 00:22:05.480
a great idea.

00:22:05.480 --> 00:22:06.712
If you don't have it, get it.

00:22:06.712 --> 00:22:08.920
It will make your life a
lot easier on the long term.

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:11.128
The businesses we see moving
really fast in migration

00:22:11.128 --> 00:22:13.190
have this capability in place.

00:22:13.190 --> 00:22:16.040
It certainly helps.

00:22:16.040 --> 00:22:18.200
And then monitoring tooling--

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:20.319
so just kind of a pro-tip here.

00:22:20.319 --> 00:22:21.860
If you're going to
monitor something,

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:24.590
don't host it on the same
service you're monitoring--

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:26.780
in case anyone
followed last week.

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:31.552
But generally if you're going
to have something, monitor it.

00:22:31.552 --> 00:22:32.510
We have stacked driver.

00:22:32.510 --> 00:22:33.050
It's a great tool.

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:34.340
There's lots of them out there.

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:35.965
But if you're going
to be in the Cloud,

00:22:35.965 --> 00:22:38.190
have that set up as part
of the migration as well.

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:39.440
And then finally, test
after you migrate.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:41.600
Make sure it's all working
and have some definition

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:43.400
of what working means to you.

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:46.976
And now let's look at what
it means to move data.

00:22:46.976 --> 00:22:49.350
Just some basic rules again,
not in any particular order.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:51.610
Data size-- it's
kind of obvious,

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:53.310
but in some ways it isn't.

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:56.130
Data size dictates difficulty.

00:22:56.130 --> 00:22:58.530
The bigger it is and the
smaller your bandwidth, the more

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:01.290
challenge you're going
to have moving data.

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:03.984
Relational schemas
tend to be easier, too.

00:23:03.984 --> 00:23:05.400
Things you're not
using very often

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:07.025
are easy to move as
well because you're

00:23:07.025 --> 00:23:10.840
dealing with the fast
incremental updates.

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:14.110
Large data sets
require planning.

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:16.840
If you're going to move--

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:19.980
Evernote mentioned moving
70 petabytes or 30 petabytes

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:20.797
in 70 days.

00:23:20.797 --> 00:23:22.130
That requires a lot of planning.

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:23.729
You don't just show
up and do that.

00:23:23.729 --> 00:23:25.770
There's only so much you
can push over the wires.

00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:27.130
There are only so many
trucks you can bring up

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:28.022
to your data center.

00:23:28.022 --> 00:23:29.980
And we like to look at
this chart as just a way

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:31.125
to think about it.

00:23:31.125 --> 00:23:32.730
To understand the
scale of the problem

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:33.850
there is some basic math here.

00:23:33.850 --> 00:23:35.600
So if you're going to
move 100-- if you're

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:39.550
going to move 100 terabytes,
roughly 25 hard drives,

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:43.156
over a 100 megabyte wire, it's
going to take you 124 days.

00:23:43.156 --> 00:23:44.530
If you can use
Cloud interconnect

00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:46.746
and get a 10 gigabyte
per second link,

00:23:46.746 --> 00:23:47.870
that same time is 30 hours.

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:49.495
And Just a dramatic
difference in terms

00:23:49.495 --> 00:23:50.837
of your ability to move things.

00:23:50.837 --> 00:23:52.420
This is up on the
Google Cloud website

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:53.950
if you guys want to
look at it and just get

00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:55.660
a sense of the scale of the
problem you're taking on.

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:56.050
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Yeah.

00:23:56.050 --> 00:23:57.940
So in case you're
curious about this chart,

00:23:57.940 --> 00:24:00.000
this is available on--

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.890
there's a solution actually
that was relatively recently

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:04.090
released maybe a month ago.

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:07.900
So it was transferring
big data sets to GCP.

00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:09.952
We literally just stole
it from that page.

00:24:09.952 --> 00:24:12.410
But then there's a whole lot
of detail about how that works

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:15.820
and different strategies and
advice on how to do that.

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:20.180
BRIAN STARK: It's probably
important to evaluate

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:22.904
the different storage
options within the Cloud.

00:24:22.904 --> 00:24:24.320
If you're not
familiar with Cloud,

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:26.440
it can be a little
bit daunting at first.

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:30.150
It was for me when
I joined Cloud.

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:32.979
And just because you use
a certain data type today

00:24:32.979 --> 00:24:35.520
doesn't mean you should use that
going forward in the future.

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:36.500
So just understand
the different options

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:38.390
between block an
object and the others,

00:24:38.390 --> 00:24:40.700
and understand what it means
for you from a performance

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:42.320
perspective, and then understand
what it means from a cost

00:24:42.320 --> 00:24:43.153
perspective as well.

00:24:46.491 --> 00:24:48.740
And then consider the data
structure you're moving to.

00:24:48.740 --> 00:24:49.880
So if you change
your storage, just

00:24:49.880 --> 00:24:50.840
be aware what the
differences are

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:52.520
and how that's going to
impact your application.

00:24:52.520 --> 00:24:54.080
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Can I
just stop you for one second?

00:24:54.080 --> 00:24:56.413
So this is actually a question
that comes up quite a bit

00:24:56.413 --> 00:24:59.191
when we are helping
customers move.

00:24:59.191 --> 00:25:01.690
This is what we're using today,
what does that translate to?

00:25:01.690 --> 00:25:03.406
We are using MySQL.

00:25:03.406 --> 00:25:04.280
We're using Postgres.

00:25:04.280 --> 00:25:06.080
We're using Cassandra.

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:09.320
How does that map to--
what do I take here

00:25:09.320 --> 00:25:10.760
that maps directly to here?

00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:14.750
And sometimes there's
an easy answer.

00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:17.410
Well, if you're using MySQL,
you can continue to use MySQL.

00:25:17.410 --> 00:25:18.660
That's fairly straightforward.

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:20.750
But sometimes it's
not an easy answer.

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:26.510
If you say we have 15
Cassandra clusters,

00:25:26.510 --> 00:25:28.280
what should I move it to?

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:30.660
The answer is probably
not any one thing.

00:25:30.660 --> 00:25:32.704
It's not necessarily
going to be Bigtable.

00:25:32.704 --> 00:25:34.370
It's not necessarily
going to be Spanner

00:25:34.370 --> 00:25:36.030
or whatever the case may be.

00:25:36.030 --> 00:25:43.220
And so finding out and
figuring out where data moves

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:45.414
can be one of the harder
parts of a migration.

00:25:45.414 --> 00:25:47.330
The actual mechanics of
doing it is actually--

00:25:47.330 --> 00:25:48.900
it can often be
not that difficult.

00:25:48.900 --> 00:25:51.210
It's the decision process
that is much more difficult.

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:54.324
And so I think most of
Google, like database products

00:25:54.324 --> 00:25:56.490
in particular-- if you go
from CloudSQL to Datastore

00:25:56.490 --> 00:26:00.020
or Bigtable, Spanner
and BigQuery in certain

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:01.280
circumstances--

00:26:01.280 --> 00:26:03.560
I think they have well
defined use cases,

00:26:03.560 --> 00:26:06.324
but they also have
cross-over use cases

00:26:06.324 --> 00:26:08.240
where there's a bit of
a gray area in between.

00:26:08.240 --> 00:26:14.300
Where you think, well, I've
got a database that is,

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:17.820
let's say, not
really relational.

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:21.032
I think it could probably fit
into Datastore or Bigtable.

00:26:21.032 --> 00:26:21.990
Which one should I use?

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:23.448
And sometimes the
answer was, well,

00:26:23.448 --> 00:26:25.260
you probably could use both.

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:28.527
And figuring out which one
is actually the best one

00:26:28.527 --> 00:26:30.110
comes down to a few
different factors.

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:31.485
It usually comes
down to how much

00:26:31.485 --> 00:26:33.986
are you going to spend on it,
where is it most optimized,

00:26:33.986 --> 00:26:36.360
how are you going to use it
for this particular use case?

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:39.570
But we often get questions like,
what is the direct mapping?

00:26:39.570 --> 00:26:41.940
Exactly how does
this map into that?

00:26:41.940 --> 00:26:42.930
What is the one thing?

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:45.840
And more often than not
there is not the one thing.

00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:47.880
There is a few things
that could work.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:50.850
So it really does take
actual evaluation and testing

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:53.177
of the particular system to
find out what works best.

00:26:53.177 --> 00:26:54.010
BRIAN STARK: Thanks.

00:26:56.132 --> 00:26:57.840
Every now and again
you make great sense.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:00.456
I appreciate you chiming in.

00:27:00.456 --> 00:27:03.574
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
So that's it, right?

00:27:03.574 --> 00:27:04.740
There's nothing else, right?

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:06.780
BRIAN STARK: No.

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:07.665
Eager beaver.

00:27:10.220 --> 00:27:11.320
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: OK.

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:12.022
Fine.

00:27:12.022 --> 00:27:13.480
Maybe I'll talk
about other things.

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:16.320
So we touched on this a
little bit in best practices

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:19.814
in terms of actually
finishing a migration.

00:27:19.814 --> 00:27:21.230
A lot of the
customers that I talk

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:24.330
to when we kind of talk
through this particular flow,

00:27:24.330 --> 00:27:27.861
and we do actually talk
about this quite a bit--

00:27:27.861 --> 00:27:30.480
is I often say, you're not done
migrating until you actually

00:27:30.480 --> 00:27:33.757
do tests, because you don't
really know what you've done

00:27:33.757 --> 00:27:35.340
or that you've been
successful at what

00:27:35.340 --> 00:27:36.790
you've done until you test it.

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:38.962
And so certainly, unit tests--

00:27:38.962 --> 00:27:40.920
if you're not doing them,
you should absolutely

00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:41.503
be doing them.

00:27:41.503 --> 00:27:43.312
But run those once
they're in there,

00:27:43.312 --> 00:27:44.520
make sure things are working.

00:27:44.520 --> 00:27:46.020
Then functional tests--
have somebody sit down

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:48.399
and say, OK, well, this is
how our web app used to work.

00:27:48.399 --> 00:27:49.440
This is how it works now.

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:49.940
Great.

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:50.490
It works.

00:27:50.490 --> 00:27:51.270
Keep using it.

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:53.417
Or-- oh, no, it
broke at like step 3.

00:27:53.417 --> 00:27:54.000
What happened?

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:55.551
We thought we set
it up the same way.

00:27:55.551 --> 00:27:58.050
Test it and make sure that it
actually works before you say,

00:27:58.050 --> 00:27:59.495
yeah, I'm done.

00:27:59.495 --> 00:28:01.620
And then I think something
that people often forget

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:03.090
is actually load testing.

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:06.270
So let's say you have this
web application that's

00:28:06.270 --> 00:28:08.610
capable of supporting
like 10,000

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:12.000
concurrent connections in
your existing infrastructure.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:13.050
Port it over.

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:14.190
Yes, it passes unit tests.

00:28:14.190 --> 00:28:15.150
Yes, it passes functional tests.

00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:16.260
It's working the same way.

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:17.280
Let it go to production.

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:21.585
And then a half hour
into real users using it,

00:28:21.585 --> 00:28:22.960
you realize that
it only supports

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:25.180
10 concurrent
connections not 10,000.

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:27.580
And that's a big difference,
and that's a big problem.

00:28:27.580 --> 00:28:29.620
So you don't really
know if it's working

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:32.940
until you figure out what its
performance capabilities are.

00:28:32.940 --> 00:28:35.770
And so a dramatic change
like that would mean probably

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:36.940
there's some sort of
configuration problem

00:28:36.940 --> 00:28:38.000
or something like that.

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:40.069
But you want to figure
that out before you say,

00:28:40.069 --> 00:28:41.110
release it to production.

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:42.944
Say, yeah, this is ready to go.

00:28:42.944 --> 00:28:44.110
We're comfortable with that.

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:49.220
And then finally-- not
finally, I'm sorry.

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:52.430
Monitoring-- again, Brian
touched on this a little bit.

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:58.370
We see a lot of companies come
in essentially flying blind.

00:28:58.370 --> 00:29:01.472
And if you're not monitoring,
if you're not using some sort

00:29:01.472 --> 00:29:02.930
of tool-- whether
it's Stackdriver,

00:29:02.930 --> 00:29:04.520
whether it's your own tool,
whether it's a third party

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:05.420
tool--

00:29:05.420 --> 00:29:07.934
there's no real way
to kind of figure out

00:29:07.934 --> 00:29:10.100
how things are moving and
how things have been done.

00:29:10.100 --> 00:29:12.512
So you really don't
know that you're done.

00:29:12.512 --> 00:29:13.970
You don't know that
the application

00:29:13.970 --> 00:29:15.560
is working unless
you can actually

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:17.390
monitor what it's doing.

00:29:17.390 --> 00:29:19.070
And again, this is
actually critical.

00:29:19.070 --> 00:29:22.340
This is essential to
finishing a migration,

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:24.180
because you don't
know it otherwise.

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:28.510
And then finally that
alerting is great,

00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:31.110
but it's not enough unless
you're actually getting--

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:32.110
the monitoring is great.

00:29:32.110 --> 00:29:34.990
It's not enough unless
you're getting alerts

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:36.860
based on what happens.

00:29:36.860 --> 00:29:38.620
So if you're
monitoring everything,

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:40.360
and you're monitoring
the right things,

00:29:40.360 --> 00:29:42.254
and the monitors go
all the way to 100%,

00:29:42.254 --> 00:29:43.670
because you're
using too much CPU,

00:29:43.670 --> 00:29:45.420
you filled up your
disk or whatever it is,

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:47.170
and nothing happens
because of that--

00:29:47.170 --> 00:29:49.480
that's kind of
only half the work.

00:29:49.480 --> 00:29:51.040
You have to be able
to alert on that.

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:54.350
You have to be able to tell
people this is becoming bad.

00:29:54.350 --> 00:29:56.659
The situation is getting worse.

00:29:56.659 --> 00:29:57.700
Action needs to be taken.

00:29:57.700 --> 00:29:59.199
Somebody needs to
take care of that.

00:29:59.199 --> 00:30:01.150
So alerting and monitoring
really very much

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:02.297
go hand-in-hand.

00:30:02.297 --> 00:30:03.880
And too often we see
kind of only half

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:07.370
of that solution in there.

00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:08.180
So how about now?

00:30:08.180 --> 00:30:10.658
Are we done?

00:30:10.658 --> 00:30:12.240
BRIAN STARK: For
now I think we are.

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:13.110
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Yeah?

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:13.776
What comes next?

00:30:16.885 --> 00:30:18.760
Well, you get the clicker
is what comes next.

00:30:18.760 --> 00:30:19.560
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:21.700
BRIAN STARK: How about
a larger migration?

00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:23.750
You ready for the next one?

00:30:23.750 --> 00:30:25.470
Seriously, migrations
can be difficult.

00:30:25.470 --> 00:30:26.345
They can be involved.

00:30:26.345 --> 00:30:27.330
They can be lengthy.

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:28.940
But they don't have
to be complicated.

00:30:28.940 --> 00:30:30.390
Whether your moving one
application or 1,000,

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:31.970
the general path is the same.

00:30:31.970 --> 00:30:33.180
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
And so this is something

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:34.530
that we've seen-- oh, sorry.

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:35.550
BRIAN STARK: No, no.

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:36.614
It's a basic process.

00:30:36.614 --> 00:30:37.530
You pick applications.

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:38.446
You move applications.

00:30:38.446 --> 00:30:39.840
You move data--
rinse and repeat.

00:30:39.840 --> 00:30:40.320
PETER-MARK VERWOERD: Yeah.

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:40.830
BRIAN STARK: You'll
get through it.

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:42.705
PETER-MARK VERWOERD:
And so this is something

00:30:42.705 --> 00:30:46.000
that we talk about over and
over with different customers.

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:47.260
And the reason why--

00:30:47.260 --> 00:30:51.570
again, this is why this topic
is moving a single application

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:54.750
rather than moving
hundreds of applications.

00:30:54.750 --> 00:30:56.610
Because actually the
workflow is the same,

00:30:56.610 --> 00:30:57.610
and this is what we see.

00:30:57.610 --> 00:31:01.140
So you take whatever
snapshot of what

00:31:01.140 --> 00:31:03.390
you have to move that makes
sense inside of your head,

00:31:03.390 --> 00:31:04.889
whether it's moving
the whole thing,

00:31:04.889 --> 00:31:07.110
whether it's moving
subsections, teams,

00:31:07.110 --> 00:31:08.610
single applications at a time.

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:11.050
It actually is pretty much the
same process over and over.

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:11.550
Sorry.

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:13.722
I stepped all over you.

00:31:13.722 --> 00:31:15.250
BRIAN STARK: But
at the end you're

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:17.360
going to end up with
a beautiful house.

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:18.780
So thank you, guys, very much.

00:31:18.780 --> 00:31:22.430
